meta {
  name: Public Address Autocomplete
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/address/{{shareLinkToken}}/autocomplete
  body: json
  auth: none
}

script:pre-request {
  // Hand-rolled rather than `crypto.randomUUID()` — Bruno's script sandbox is
  // QuickJS and has no `require`. The API only asks for 8-64 opaque characters.
  bru.setVar(
    "publicAddressSessionToken",
    "bru-pub-" +
      Math.random().toString(36).slice(2) +
      Math.random().toString(36).slice(2),
  );
}

body:json {
  {
    "input": "4821 North Maple",
    "sessionToken": "{{publicAddressSessionToken}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK without any Authorization header", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("carries the same { available, suggestions } envelope as the authenticated route", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.available).to.be.a("boolean");
    expect(body.suggestions).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("never leaks the API key or Google's raw payload", function () {
    const raw = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(raw).to.not.include("AIza");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("addressComponents");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("googleapis.com");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /public/address/:token/autocomplete — address lookup on the public form (PAC-60)

  The same type-ahead as `POST /address/autocomplete`, reachable from the
  unauthenticated share-link intake form. **Public** (`@Public()`): no JWT, no
  `AccessContext`, and all six global guards bypassed.

  ## Every check the guard chain would have made is repeated by hand
  Via `ShareLinkAccessService.resolve` — the *same* implementation
  `GET /public/lead-form/:token` and `POST /public/leads/:token` use:

  1. token shape (43-char base64url — a cheap pre-DB filter, **not** a security control)
  2. the link exists and is active
  3. the agency exists and is `active`
  4. the `leads` module is enabled for that agency
  5. the link's producer is still an active user

  Every rejection returns the **same** `404 This form is no longer available.`,
  so the endpoint cannot be used to learn whether a token ever existed, whether
  it was revoked, or which agency it belonged to. It was extracted into a shared
  service rather than copied for exactly this reason: a second hand-written
  implementation drifts, and the way it drifts is by becoming more helpful.
  Pinned by `packages/api/test/public-address.e2e-spec.ts`.

  ## Why the browser cannot just call Google directly
  This endpoint is the whole reason PAC-60 proxies Places instead of dropping in
  `<gmp-place-autocomplete>`. That element needs an API key **in the browser**,
  and this form is an anonymous share link — the key would be trivially scraped.
  HTTP-referrer restriction is the usual mitigation, but the referrer here is by
  definition public and spoofable.

  ## Three layers of spend control
  | Layer | Guards against | Where |
  |---|---|---|
  | Quota cap on the Google key | everything; the only **hard** stop (budgets merely alert) | GCP console |
  | Per-IP `@Throttle` | one machine hammering | this route |
  | **Per-share-link daily cap** | one scraped link driven from **many** IPs | `ShareLink.addressLookupCount` |

  The third is the one that matters. Per-IP throttling structurally cannot see a
  distributed hit on a single link, and every request is billed by Google.

  ⚠ **Over the per-link cap the answer is `200 { available: false }`, not `429`** —
  a deliberate difference from the intake limits. The client latches
  `available: false` and stops asking for the rest of the page session, the
  submitter types the address by hand, and the form still submits. A 429 would
  paint an error on a form that is working fine. A **lost lead is the expensive
  outcome here, not a lost autocomplete.**

  ## Response `200`
  Identical shape to the authenticated route:
  ```json
  { "available": true, "suggestions": [ { "placeId": "ChIJ...", "primaryText": "...", "secondaryText": "..." } ] }
  ```

  ## ⚠ Tests assert the envelope, never real predictions
  A dev machine will usually have no `GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY`, so this answers
  `{ "available": false, "suggestions": [] }` and that is a pass.

  ## Rate limiting
  `30 / minute` and `200 / hour` per IP (`PUBLIC_ADDRESS_RATE_LIMIT`,
  `PUBLIC_ADDRESS_HOURLY_LIMIT`), plus `500 / day` per share link
  (`PUBLIC_ADDRESS_LINK_DAILY_LIMIT`).
  ⚠ The first two must be **real environment variables** — `@Throttle` bakes
  them into decorator metadata before `ConfigModule` runs dotenv.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — `input` under 3 characters, or a missing/oversized `sessionToken`.
  - `404 Not Found` — any problem with the link, agency, module or producer. One message for all of them.
  - `429 Too Many Requests` — per-IP rate limit exceeded.

  ## Run order
  After **Leads › Share Links › Create Share Link**, which sets `shareLinkToken`.
}
